"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[61328],{961328:e=>{e.exports='# Genie Space Metadata Bootstrap\n\n**USE THIS SKILL** when the user asks to:\n\n- "Make this new Genie space high quality"\n- "Bootstrap this space" or "set up this space"\n- "Auto-configure metadata for this space"\n- Target a specific bootstrap aspect, e.g. "add descriptions to columns that are missing them" or "add JOIN relationships between the tables in this space"\n\nThis skill is a one-shot bootstrap. Always ask for explicit author confirmation before applying any changes.\n\n## Context\n\nThe user is a Genie space author who wants to quickly populate a quality baseline of metadata for their space by pulling from Unity Catalog and existing workspace query activity. The space may have attached tables but no descriptions, no JOIN knowledge snippets, and no example SQL yet.\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Step 1: Read the space config\n\nCall `readSpaceConfig` to get the list of attached tables, the current name and description, warehouse, and run-as type.\n\n### Step 2: Read per-table schema and UC metadata\n\nFor each attached table FQN, call `readTable` with `extraDetails: true`. Issue these calls in parallel.\n\nEach response carries:\n\n- Column types and comments under `columns`\n- Declared constraints under `metadata.primary_keys` (array of `{name, columns}`) and `metadata.foreign_keys` (array of `{toTable, toTableColumns, columns}`)\n- Observed historical joins in `topJoins` and frequent queries in `topQueries`\n- Per-column UC metadata inside each column\'s `type_json` string. After parsing, look under `metadata`:\n  - `metadata["metric_view.type"]`: `dimension` or `measure` for metric-view columns\n  - `metadata["metric_view.expr"]`: the SQL expression that defines a measure or derived dimension\n  - `metadata.display_name`: any existing UI display name\n  - `metadata.synonyms`: existing synonyms list\n\nIf a `readTable` call fails for a table in the space (permission denied, not found, etc.), record it as inaccessible. Do not attempt to configure metadata for it, and surface it in the summary to the author.\n\n### Step 3: Fetch popular SQL across the space\n\nCall `getPopularQueries`. Returns a space-wide ranking of recently-executed SQL that references the attached tables, with SQL text, execution frequency, and which tables it referenced. Use for:\n\n- Example SQL candidates to promote into the space\n- JOIN relationships observable in query history but not declared as constraints\n- Column synonyms and business aliases, e.g. `SELECT sls_cmp_1 AS "Sales Complete"`\n\n### Step 4: Find relevant saved queries and assets\n\nLook beyond execution history for SQL and assets that are semantically aligned with the space. Run these in parallel:\n\n- `querySearch`: search saved SQL queries by keywords. Build keyword sets from (a) the space name and description, (b) the attached table names and column display names / synonyms, and (c) any obvious business domain terms. Issue multiple targeted searches rather than one broad one.\n- `searchAssets`: search dashboards, notebooks, pipelines, jobs, and files using the same keyword sets. Useful for surfacing canonical dashboards or notebooks that already encode the business questions the space is meant to answer.\n- `findReferencesTool`: for each attached table FQN, find downstream lineage references. This pulls saved queries, dashboards, and notebooks that actually read these tables, regardless of keyword match.\n- `forYouAssets`: understand what tables, dashboards, and queries the user has recently used — look for assets related to the space\'s current tables or description\n\n**Relevance filter (apply before proposing anything):** a saved query is only a candidate if at least one of these holds:\n\n1. **Table relevance**: the query references one or more of the attached tables (verified by parsing the SQL or by `findReferencesTool` lineage). Queries that only reference unrelated tables are out, even if keywords match.\n2. **Business-semantic relevance**: the query name, description, or SQL clearly maps to the space\'s stated domain (from the space name, description, and table set). Reject queries that are generic (e.g. ad-hoc one-offs, debug queries, schema introspection) or that target a different domain.\n\nDrop everything that fails both checks. When in doubt, drop it — a small set of clearly-relevant queries is better than a large set with noise. De-duplicate against `getPopularQueries` results from Step 3 and against existing SQL examples found in Step 5 (`readInstructions`).\n\n### Step 5: Read existing instructions\n\nCall `readInstructions` to list text instructions, SQL examples, and knowledge snippets already in the space, along with their IDs and types. Use this to avoid proposing duplicates.\n\n### Step 6: Draft and propose\n\nSynthesize the findings into a change plan and present it for approval, organized by action type:\n\n- **Descriptions**: tables and columns that have UC comments the space is not yet surfacing.\n- **JOINs**: declared FK constraints first, then additional JOINs inferred from `topJoins`, JOIN clauses in `getPopularQueries`, and JOIN clauses in the relevant saved queries from Step 4.\n- **Example SQL**: domain-relevant queries from `getPopularQueries` plus the filtered saved queries from Step 4. Each candidate must pass the Step 4 relevance filter — never propose a saved query that only matched on keywords without referencing the attached tables or clearly mapping to the space\'s domain.\n- **Columns to hide**: low-value columns (internal IDs, audit columns with no description, sparse or unrelated columns). Be conservative and lean toward keeping when uncertain. Do not hide MEASURE columns in metric views; DIMENSIONs are the primary join/filter targets.\n- **Synonyms to preserve or add**: verify existing `metadata.synonyms` on columns so the author knows they will not be overwritten.\n- **Entity matching configuration**: Ensure frequently referenced columns have entity matching enabled.\n\nExplicitly call out inaccessible tables from Step 2 and note that the agent skipped them.\n\nIf the user targeted only one aspect (e.g. just JOINs), limit the plan to that slice.\n\n### Step 7: Apply changes in order, after explicit confirmation\n\n1. Hide low-value columns → `updateColumnVisibility`.\n2. Genie Space description → `updateSpaceConfig`.\n3. Column descriptions → `useUpdateColumnDescriptions`\n4. JOIN relationships from declared FKs → `addKnowledgeSnippetsToSpace` (type `join`). Then additional inferred JOINs.\n5. Example SQL → `addInstructionsToSpace` (type `sql_example`).\n6. Column synonyms → `useUpdateColumnSynonymsTool`.\n7. Entity matching configuration → `useConfigureEntityMatching`.\n\n## Optional clarifications\n\nLean toward action, but a few cases are worth pausing for:\n\n- **Raw/bronze-layer warning**: if attached tables look like non-aggregated raw data (poorly named columns, complex types, no clear domain), warn the author and recommend pre-modeling. If a well-formed metric view on the same domain exists, suggest attaching it instead.\n- **Ambiguous business domain**: if the tables do not clearly indicate a domain, ask for a one-liner on the space\'s purpose plus 1-2 example questions before continuing.\n- **Ambiguous columns**: for frequently used columns that remain unclear after reading UC context and popular SQL, ask briefly for clarification.\n\nDefault is to act. Only ask when really necessary.\n\n## Output\n\n1. **Thinking trace** (brief): what context was retrieved, what was found, what was chosen and why.\n2. **Final summary**: tables and columns configured, JOINs added (counts and which tables), example SQL added (counts), columns hidden with per-column reasons, inaccessible tables skipped.\n3. A follow-up link `[Go to benchmarks tab](#followup)` so the author can validate the space against benchmark questions.\n\n## Important notes\n\n- Always ask for confirmation before applying changes. This is a one-shot bootstrap and over-eager edits are hard to undo.\n- Preserve existing author-authored descriptions and synonyms. Only fill gaps, never overwrite.\n- When no data is available from UC or query history, say so explicitly and stop rather than inventing configurations.\n'}}]);
